<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Busoga principalities</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1967 CE (approx.) · capital Jinja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A cluster of small Basoga principalities east of the Nile, united under the British as a kingdom with an elected ruler, the Kyabazinga.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From about the 16th century the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-kyoga">Lake Kyoga</a> was divided among numerous small principalities, many of whose ruling families traced their origins to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Bunyoro</a> and its legendary hunter Mukama. No central kingdom emerged, and the principalities often paid tribute to the more powerful <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each principality was ruled by a hereditary chief with clan heads and village leaders. The economy rested on plantains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and fishing on the lakes. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uganda-protectorate">Uganda Protectorate</a> the British placed Busoga under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Ganda</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semei-kakungulu">Semei Kakungulu</a> in 1906, then created a council of chiefs and, in 1919, the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyabazinga">Kyabazinga</a>, chosen among the senior chiefs, to head Busoga as a single unit from Bugembe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyabazinga">Kyabazinga's</a> office was abolished with the other Ugandan monarchies in 1967 and restored as a cultural institution in 1995. Kyabazinga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce-nadiope">William Wilberforce Nadiope</a> served as Uganda's vice-president in the 1960s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/busoga-principalities.jpg" alt="Busoga principalities" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Busoga_(royal_standard).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1967 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jinja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Wilberforce Nadiope</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, later Christianity and Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lusoga</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Busoga, Kingdom of Busoga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1734&amp;focus=busoga-principalities" aria-label="Busoga principalities on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5603 2160 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Busoga principalities lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5923 2416L5900 2421M5923 2416L5902 2432"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5900 2421h0M5902 2432h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5923 2416h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5923 2416h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1734&amp;focus=busoga-principalities">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

